module gunbc.emitted_subject_build_gate

import std.types { String, List }
import extdeps.bazel.label { Label, render_label }
import extdeps.exec.command { ArgvCommand }
import gunbc.gunbc_cli_command { gunbc_test_command }
import gunbc.instrument_targets { self_host_label, v2_native_cli_label }

// THE SUBJECT OF THE REQUIRED EMIT-AND-BUILD LANE, AND WHAT IT IS CAREFULLY NOT.
//
// WHAT IT IS. Two instruments, each of which asks the seed to EMIT a retained v2 closure and then
// asks cargo to BUILD the emitted crate: `//gunbc/instruments:self-host` over `v2.compiler.compile`
// and `//gunbc/instruments:v2-native-cli` over `v2.cli.compile_cli`. Both also start what they
// built -- the self-host row's census-mode pair, the CLI row's `cli_no_entry` refusal arm -- and
// the self-host row injects a type error into its own emitted module and requires cargo to fail
// alone on a diagnostic naming it, so a fingerprint alias or a replayed cached verdict cannot stand
// in for a compile. Those controls are the instruments' own and are not restated here; this module
// decides only WHICH subjects the required lane runs and with what argv.
//
// WHAT ITS RED DISCRIMINATES THAT NO EXISTING REQUIRED LANE DOES, which is the question
// gunbc.compiler_gate_workflow's roster header requires an author to answer before proposing a
// lane. Every required lane today reads the corpus through the INTERPRETER or through the SEED's
// own Rust: the floor evaluates witnesses, the clippy lane lints hand-written and stage0-mirrored
// Rust, the compiler lane builds the seed binary. NOTHING ON THE MERGE PATH EMITS A V2 CLOSURE AND
// COMPILES THE RESULT. So a defect that is invisible under evaluation and fatal under emission
// reaches main green. That is not a hypothesis: `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode`
// `bounded_natural_arithmetic_evaluated_as_unbounded_int` records it in as many words -- rows over
// `UInt8` typecheck and their witnesses evaluate, "their emitted Rust is produced by nothing on the
// merge path" -- and BOTH retained subjects refused on it at once, because `std.integer` is in every
// closure. It happened again on 2026-09-21 when gunbc#12004 pulled `base16` into the closure and
// broke main's native self-host build with no required check noticing.
//
// THE CAUSE IS NAMED CORRECTLY HERE BECAUSE THE FIRST READING OF IT WAS WRONG, and the wrong one
// is the one that reads plausibly. The emitted `extdeps_numeric_base16.rs` refused with E0425
// `UInt8 not found in this scope`, which looks exactly like a dropped import and was first
// recorded here as "a latent emitter import gap". It was not: `extdeps.numeric.base16` was
// SPELLING THE UNINHABITED `UInt8` CARRIER where values are put, and the repair (gunbc#12056,
// sharp-bear-756) carries `std.integer` `QualifiedOctets` through its codec surface instead --
// the emitter is untouched by it. This is the same rostered class the paragraph above cites,
// not a second defect.
//
// A DROPPED-USE-LINE EMITTER DEFECT DOES EXIST and is deliberately not conflated with this one:
// an authored `import M { T }` emits no use-line when two modules in the closure declare the
// short spelling, the named declaration is a non-nominal alias, and the second declarer arrives
// through a module the consumer does not import. It is real, reproduced under a one-variable
// experiment, and UNFIXED -- so this lane will meet it again on any module satisfying all three,
// and a reader must not treat base16's repair as having closed it.
//
// This lane is the check that would have noticed, and its red is structural to the diff --
// it names the emitted module and the compiler diagnostic -- rather than a host or shared-runner
// signal.
//
// WHAT IT IS NOT, AND THE NAMING IS LOAD-BEARING. This lane is EMIT-AND-BUILD. It is not the native
// ROUTE, and it touches no line of `gunbc.rung_drop` `v2_native_route_off_the_merge_path`'s declared
// population -- every one of those five lines is route adjudication or execution of the derived
// `v2.test.*` universe by the emitted-native compiler, and nothing here executes that universe,
// mints a `NativeRouteReceipt`, or evaluates a clause of `native_route_admission`. That drop stays
// STANDING and UNNARROWED, and its restoration trigger is untouched by this change. The lane is
// therefore named for emit-and-build rather than for the native route, so a reader meeting the
// green does not read it as coverage of the drop: a true sentence under a name that suggests more
// is rung inflation by naming (DESIGN section 4b(1)), and the cheapest place to prevent it is the
// name.

// ONE SUBJECT PER LABEL, AND TWO LABELS RATHER THAN ONE. `gunbc.instrument_targets` states why
// these are two instruments and not one: the two closures are DIFFERENT rather than nested, neither
// contains the other's `compiler_pipeline_entry`, and the CLI closure reaches modules
// `v2.compiler.compile`'s does not. A green on one is not evidence about the other. The lane
// therefore runs them as two STEPS with two step ids, so a regression is reported under a name that
// says which compilation broke -- the same reason the labels are two.
type EmittedSubjectBuildRow {
  label: Label
  step_id: String
}

// THE STEP ID IS A ROW FIELD, NOT A TRANSFORMATION OF THE LABEL. A GitHub step id and a Bazel
// label are two different namespaces with two different alphabets -- a label carries `/` and `:`,
// neither of which an id admits -- so deriving one from the other means authoring a mangling rule
// whose only consumer is this list. Two rows say the same thing with no rule to maintain, and the
// pairing stays visible at the one place a subject is added.
fn emitted_subject_build_rows() -> List<EmittedSubjectBuildRow> {
  [
    EmittedSubjectBuildRow { label: self_host_label(), step_id: "emit_build_self_host" },
    EmittedSubjectBuildRow { label: v2_native_cli_label(), step_id: "emit_build_v2_native_cli" },
  ]
}

// THE LABEL TEXT IS RENDERED FROM THE DECLARATION, never typed. `render_label` is the upstream
// spelling authority (extdeps.bazel.label); a step that spelled `//gunbc/instruments:self-host`
// itself would be a second name for a target this repository already declares (DESIGN section 3).
fn emitted_subject_build_label_text(row: EmittedSubjectBuildRow) -> String {
  render_label(l: row.label)
}

fn emitted_subject_build_command(row: EmittedSubjectBuildRow) -> ArgvCommand {
  gunbc_test_command(label: emitted_subject_build_label_text(row: row))
}

// THE STEP NAME CARRIES THE LABEL, for the same reason the labels are two: a run log naming only
// "emit and build" would not say which compilation refused.
fn emitted_subject_build_step_name(row: EmittedSubjectBuildRow) -> String {
  join(["emit and build ", emitted_subject_build_label_text(row: row)], "")
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE LANE'S STANDING, AND WHY IT IS A TYPED ROW RATHER THAN A SENTENCE IN A STEP
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// FROM THE MOMENT THIS LANE LANDED, EVERY PULL REQUEST IN THE REPOSITORY RUNS IT -- and while main
// cannot self-host, every one of them goes RED for a defect the author did not introduce and
// cannot fix. That is a standing cost this lane imposes on every other lane, and it is paid by
// whoever clicks the red X expecting to find their own mistake.
//
// The manager made exactly that mistake on gunbc#12043 with ONE instance in front of him: he read
// `mergeStateStatus: UNSTABLE`, took the red as a blocker, and dropped the PR from the merge card.
// It is now offered to every lane at once.
//
// WHY A TYPED STANDING AND NOT PROSE IN THE STEP. A note saying "this red is expected" is TRUE
// only while it is, and a lane still printing it after main self-hosts would be a lie that nobody
// owns -- the inert-annotation failure, on the one surface a stranger actually reads. The standing
// is a row, so retiring it is a visible edit with a name, and `EmitBuildExpectedGreen` makes the
// reassurance UNPRINTABLE the moment it stops being true.
//
// THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE HALF THAT MATTERS, and the reassurance alone would be worse than
// nothing. A standing red teaches every lane to ignore this lane, which is how a detector dies
// without anyone deciding to kill it. So the row carries the KNOWN SIGNATURE and the message tells
// a reader what would be a genuinely new finding: a different error code, or a different emitted
// file. Matching red -- noise, do nothing. Different red -- a second defect, report it.
type NativeEmitBuildStanding
  = EmitBuildExpectedGreen
  | EmitBuildExpectedRed {
      known_signature: String
      owned_by: String
      retires_when: String
    }

// THE ONE ROW TO EDIT WHEN MAIN SELF-HOSTS AGAIN. Flipping it to `EmitBuildExpectedGreen` deletes
// the notice from every future run; leaving it stale keeps printing a reassurance for a red that
// is no longer expected, which is why the type has only two arms and no "probably fine" middle.
data emit_build_standing: NativeEmitBuildStanding = EmitBuildExpectedRed {
  known_signature: "E0573 PointerWidth at src/std_integer.rs",
  owned_by: "gunbc#12070",
  retires_when: "a head of main on which `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:self-host` and `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:v2-native-cli` both exit zero"
}

// THE LINES A LANE READS WHEN IT CLICKS THE RED X. Rendered from the row above, so the reassurance
// and the discriminator cannot drift apart from the standing they describe.
fn emit_build_standing_notice() -> List<String> {
  match emit_build_standing {
    EmitBuildExpectedGreen => [
      "emit-build is a NON-REQUIRED detector lane. A red here does NOT block your merge:",
      "the required context is `witnesses`, and this job is not an input to it.",
      "No standing break is declared, so a red here is a REAL FINDING -- most likely yours.",
    ]
    EmitBuildExpectedRed { known_signature: sig, owned_by: owner, retires_when: _ } => [
      "emit-build is a NON-REQUIRED detector lane. A red here does NOT block your merge:",
      "the required context is `witnesses`, and this job is not an input to it.",
      "",
      join(["A RED IS CURRENTLY EXPECTED. main cannot self-host; the break is owned by ", owner], ""),
      join(["and its signature is: ", sig], ""),
      "",
      "WHAT TO DO: if the failure above matches that signature, this is not your defect and",
      "there is nothing for you to fix. Do not patch around it in your branch.",
      "",
      "WHAT IS A REAL FINDING: a DIFFERENT error code, or a different emitted file. That is a",
      "second defect this lane just caught, and it should be reported rather than assumed to be",
      "the known one -- every break at this step shares cause=EmittedCompilerBuildFailed, so the",
      "cause line does not discriminate and the ERROR CODE is what you compare.",
    ]
  }
}
